<interrogationes unitas="componens">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I build a component tree for a widget?
    <responsum symbolum="componens_creare componens_ponere_fines componens_addere_liberum componens_ponere_actio componens_ponere_titulum">Create
      each node with componens_creare(piscina, intern, id, partes),
      give it bounds with componens_ponere_fines (in the PARENT's
      coordinate space) and attach it with componens_addere_liberum.
      componens_ponere_actio names the action it triggers and
      componens_ponere_titulum sets its accessible label. A node is
      logical only (id, action, kind, bounds, hit predicate, focus,
      children); drawing is derived from the tree elsewhere.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why do the componens setters take a C string, and do I have to keep it alive?
    <responsum symbolum="componens_ponere_actio internare_aut_vacua chorda_internare_ex_literis" via="lib/componens.worklog.md">No:
      id, actio, titulus and spatium are interned through the node's
      InternamentumChorda when set, so the node keeps its own canonical
      copy. Aliasing the caller's literal would need a const cast that
      -Wcast-qual refuses. Because the interner refuses "", an empty
      string becomes a zero-length chorda over a static byte (not
      NIHIL), so test these fields with chorda_vacua.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I pass NIHIL as the interner to componens_creare?
    <responsum symbolum="componens_creare internare_aut_vacua chorda_internare_ex_literis internamentum_globale">You
      can, but every string is then lost without a warning: the
      setters go through chorda_internare_ex_literis, which returns
      NIHIL when there is no interner, and that NIHIL is stored as an
      empty chorda, so id, actio, titulus and spatium all come out
      empty and componens_invenire_per_id cannot find a node by name.
      Pass a real interner (internamentum_globale or your own); each
      node keeps the one it was created with.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find a component by its id?
    <responsum symbolum="componens_invenire_per_id componens_invenire_in_spatio">componens_invenire_per_id
      searches depth first, parent before children, and returns the
      first node whose id has the same bytes, or NIHIL. It ignores
      spatia, so when the same app is mounted twice use
      componens_invenire_in_spatio(arbor, spatium, id): it finds the
      root of that spatium and searches inside it without entering
      nested spatia (an empty spatium means the whole tree).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is a spatium on a component?
    <responsum symbolum="componens_ponere_spatium componens_spatium componens_invenire_in_spatio">A
      namespace for one mounted application (vicus side panes): the
      host marks the root of each mount with componens_ponere_spatium,
      and ids, actions and shapes inside resolve within it, so two
      copies of the same app do not collide. componens_spatium returns
      the nearest spatium walking up through the parents, or "" for the
      host.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I round-trip a component tree through STML without losing anything?
    <responsum symbolum="componens_scribere_stml componens_legere_stml componens_aequalis">Not
      quite. componens_scribere_stml writes id, partes, actio, titulus,
      bounds, praedicatum, focusabilis, sectio, translation, scala,
      puncta and children, and componens_legere_stml reads them back,
      but the claudit and spatium fields are not written, and
      componens_aequalis does not compare them either, so their loss
      is invisible. An unknown partes reads as PARTES_NULLUM and an
      unknown praedicatum as PRAEDICATUM_RECTANGULUM.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I make a component hit-test as a polygon or a mask?
    <responsum symbolum="componens_ponere_praedicatum componens_ponere_fines" via="lib/destinatio.c">Not
      yet. PRAEDICATUM_POLYGONUM and PRAEDICATUM_MASCA are stored and
      serialized, but the hit test in destinatio checks only the
      rectangle in fines (plus sectio clipping, translation and scala).
      PRAEDICATUM_PROPRIUS is what scriba and pictor use: the
      rectangle is hit and the component resolves the local point
      itself. There is no setter for the polygon; callers fill puncta
      and numerus_punctorum directly.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I scroll or zoom the children of a component?
    <responsum symbolum="componens_ponere_transformatio componens_ponere_sectio">componens_ponere_transformatio(c,
      x, y, scala) sets a translation and an integer scale applied to
      the children (a scala below I is raised to I), and
      componens_ponere_sectio(c, VERUM) clips children to the node's
      bounds, so points outside them never reach a child.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
